  "notes": "A transitive godan verb meaning to communicate indirectly. The kanji {仄|ほの} means 'faint' or 'dim,' giving the word a nuance of something only dimly visible or barely perceptible.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {辞職|じしょく}を{仄|ほの}めかす: to hint at resignation\n- {不満|ふまん}を{仄|ほの}めかす: to hint at dissatisfaction\n- {引退|いんたい}を{仄|ほの}めかす: to hint at retirement\n- それとなく{仄|ほの}めかす: to subtly suggest\n\nUSAGE:\nOften used in news reporting and formal contexts to describe indirect communication by public figures. The word carries a somewhat literary or formal tone compared to casual expressions like ちらっと{言|い}う.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {匂|にお}わせる: to hint, to suggest — more colloquial; often used for deliberately dropping hints, especially on social media\n- {示唆|しさ}する: to suggest, to imply — more formal and academic; used in official or analytical contexts\n- {暗示|あんじ}する: to imply, to allude to — emphasizes hidden or symbolic meaning",
